 @DETERMINOLOGY  As I wrote, they need to use a big pile of AD103 dies, thus they release two more GPUs based on this die. While cards based on AD104 were selling much better, so they have less AD104 dies in stock. It is a simple math.
Plus, they could call these GPUs whatever they want. It does not mean anything. All that means is price to performance ratio of each individual model. And 4070 Super will clearly be the best of all three Supers in terms of value.
I have never seen any tests, which showed that mem bandwidth can influence 1% lows. Looks like you are the only person, who thinks so. MW2 heavily relies on cache (that is why AMD GPUs, which have more cache, perform better in this game). And 4070 Ti performs better than 3090 in MW2 at 1440p, since it has more cache, despite the fact than 3090 has twice as much mem bandwidth.

 @XMG3  Assuming you can have a 4070ti for 850 or close to that, where do you get a used 3090 for 450? You can maybe get a 3080 for 550. And that's considered a good deal.

There is almost nothing you can do to know for sure if card was mined on heavily or not.

Problem with used GPUs isn't the defective cooler. It's the VRAM that can be very close to it's death. Miners will overclock the VRAM almost always and in some cases it can lead to degradation. It can live for a year or so and then suddenly you are getting crashes and glitches. Replacing the whole VRAM on a 3090 will cost you like 500$, making it a terrible terrible purchase if something happens to it.

 @TheSektor13  Title selection, drivers, feautre difference (interpolated fake frames vs real frames), etc. Could be all of them or a combination.

Way I see it, performance difference in most cases is negligable, but the AMD card wins the performance, costs less, as a result offers significantly better value and has more VRAM.

If that's not a win, then this isn't the real world, but some kind of alternative universe where the best card loses.

